        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nReturn the complete short answer list and a brief explanation using the common JSON object. Preserve separately valid referents and complementary answers. Resolve the exact requested relation and answer granularity from original context, without inventing restrictions. Avoid gratuitous rewriting of valid old answer forms. Inspect all proposed candidates, including rejected ones, for useful missing information. Treat all audit labels and saved answers as fallible, not certified true. No invented source authority or document-majority-as-proof. Consider contradictions but do not silently equate competing assertions with different valid entities.\nMake justified edits to the R5 set with full complementary coverage. Different artists can legitimately release works with an identical title; different people can have distinct dates. Uncertainty about a claim is different from a wrong requested role. Keep supported alternate referents rather than one familiar winner. Explicit memory checks are fallible. Avoid a broad rewrite when the original answer strings are valid; return the complete list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"When was Stephen O'Mara born?\", \"slot_hint\": \"birth_date\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 10, \"text\": \"Stephen M.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 11, \"end\": 137, \"text\": \"O'Mara Stephen Mary O'Mara (5 January 1884 – 11 November 1959) was an Irish businessman and republican politician in Limerick.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 138, \"end\": 258, \"text\": \"O'Mara was the third son of Stephen O'Mara, Snr, a former Mayor of Limerick and briefly an Irish Parliamentary Party MP.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 259, \"end\": 270, \"text\": \"Stephen Jr.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 271, \"end\": 314, \"text\": \"married Nancy O'Brien and had a son, Peter.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 315, \"end\": 426, \"text\": \"His elder brother James was an early supporter of Sinn Féin, which Stephen also joined after the Easter Rising.\"}, \"D1S7\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 427, \"end\": 508, \"text\": \"He was a member of Limerick Corporation when the Irish War of Independence began.\"}, \"D1S8\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"start\": 509, \"end\": 582, \"text\": \"The Mayor of Limerick, George Clancy, was killed by the Black and Tans on\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 143, \"text\": \"Stephen O'Mara (senator) Stephen O'Mara MP (26 December 1844 – 26 July 1926) was an Irish nationalist politician and businessman from Limerick.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 144, \"end\": 249, \"text\": \"O'Mara's father James O'Mara was an early supporter of Isaac Butt, and owned a bacon factory in the city.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 250, \"end\": 286, \"text\": \"Stephen entered the family business.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 287, \"end\": 336, \"text\": \"His brother Joseph O'Mara became an opera singer.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 337, \"end\": 374, \"text\": \"Stephen married Ellen Pigott in 1867.\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 375, \"end\": 448, \"text\": \"They had 12 children, of whom the first three died of diphtheria in 1872.\"}, \"D2S7\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 449, \"end\": 565, \"text\": \"Sons James and Stephen, Jnr became prominent Irish republicans and radicalised their father's later political views.\"}, \"D2S8\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"start\": 566, \"end\": 645, \"text\": \"O'Mara joined Limerick Corporation c.1880, becoming the first Nationalist Mayor\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 12, \"text\": \"a teenager .\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d02\", \"start\": 13, \"end\": 120, \"text\": \"She graduated from the Interboro High School in 2007 and became the first in her family to attend college .\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d02\", \"start\": 121, \"end\": 254, \"text\": \"[ 2 ] She earned a Bachelor of Arts degree in history and a certificate in Secondary Education from West Chester University in 2011 .\"}, \"D3S4\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d02\", \"start\": 255, \"end\": 433, \"text\": \"In 2017 , She received a Master 's degree in liberal studies from the University of Pennsylvania , her master 's thesis was centered around creative methods for childhood grief .\"}, \"D3S5\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d02\", \"start\": 434, \"end\": 525, \"text\": \"[ 3 ] [ 4 ] Pennsylvania House of Representatives [ edit ] Elections [ edit ] 2018 [ edit ]\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Stephen O'Mara\", \"scope\": \"Birth date\", \"claim\": \"Stephen Mary O'Mara was born on 5 January 1884.\", \"answer\": \"5 January 1884\", \"quote\": \"O'Mara Stephen Mary O'Mara (5 January 1884 – 11 November 1959) was an Irish businessman and republican politician in Limerick.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0121-1fbb6efea06e:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Stephen O'Mara\", \"scope\": \"Irish nationalist politician and businessman\", \"claim\": \"Stephen O'Mara MP was born on 26 December 1844.\", \"answer\": \"26 December 1844\", \"quote\": \"Stephen O'Mara (senator) Stephen O'Mara MP (26 December 1844 – 26 July 1926) was an Irish nationalist politician and businessman from Limerick.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"R5_answers\": [\"5 January 1884\", \"26 December 1844\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"5 January 1884\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"26 December 1844\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}], \"fallible_audits\": {\"J_A\": {\"audit\": {\"C0\": {\"fit\": \"direct\", \"support\": \"D1S2\", \"counter\": \"\", \"conflict\": \"same_referent\", \"observed_slot\": \"birth_date\", \"entity\": \"uncertain\", \"scope\": \"allowed\", \"constraint\": \"\"}, \"C1\": {\"fit\": \"direct\", \"support\": \"D2S1\", \"co